FetchSlide-v4 is a goal-conditioned robotic manipulation task. A 7-DoF Fetch arm must strike/push an object so it slides across the table and stops near a distant target.

The native observation is a dict with keys:
- observation: robot + object kinematics (25 dims)
- achieved_goal: current object position in 3D (3 dims)
- desired_goal: target object position in 3D (3 dims)

In this project, flatten_observation is enabled, so the policy input is a 31-dimensional vector:
- state[0:25] = observation
- state[25:28] = achieved_goal
- state[28:31] = desired_goal

The action space is Box(-1, 1, (4,), float32):
- action[0]: x-axis Cartesian control of the gripper
- action[1]: y-axis Cartesian control of the gripper
- action[2]: z-axis Cartesian control of the gripper
- action[3]: gripper open/close command

The policy is a linear policy with 128 parameters and works as follows:
action = state @ W + b, where
- state has shape (31,)
- W has shape (31, 4)
- b has shape (4,)

Parameter mapping:
- W[i, j] = params[4 * i + j], for i in [0..30], j in [0..3]
- b[j] = params[124 + j], for j in [0..3]

Equivalent grouped view:
- params[0..3]     = weights from state[0] to 4 actions
- params[4..7]     = weights from state[1] to 4 actions
- ...
- params[120..123] = weights from state[30] to 4 actions
- params[124..127] = action biases

With reward_type=dense, reward is shaped by object-to-goal distance and is highest near 0 when the object reaches the target.
The objective is to maximize cumulative episodic reward by producing smooth and well-timed interactions so the object slides accurately to the goal.
